2. The obligations and responsibilities of the regional government as referred to in Article 5 letter a include:a. implementing policies for the protection of women and children victims of violence based on laws and regulations;
b. establishing policies, programs, and activities for the protection of women and children victims of violence;
c. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection of women and children victims of violence;
d. facilitating assistance, legal aid and legal services according to the needs of victims;
e. allocating budget for the implementation of protection of women and children from victims of violence according to regional financial capacity; and;
f. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection of women and children victims of violence.
3. In order to carry out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regent establishes programs and action activities for the protection of women and children in one Regional Action Plan as a basis for OPD in implementing the protection of women and children from acts of violence.
4. The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (3), is part of the Regional Medium-Term Development Plan (RPJMD).

Article 7

1. The obligations and responsibilities of the community as referred to in Article 5 letter b, are carried out in the form of community participation. (2) The form of community participation as referred to in paragraph (1), includes:a. preventing the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children;
b. providing information and/or reporting acts of violence against women and children to law enforcement or authorized parties;
c. participating in handling victims of acts of violence;
d. providing protection to victims; ande. providing emergency assistance.

jdih.pakpakbharatkab.go.id - 7 -

CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Article 2

Protection of women and children victims of violence is organized based on the principles of:
a. respect for human rights;
b. gender equality and justice;
c. non-discriminatory;
d. protection of victims' rights;
e. legal certainty.

Article 13

SPM as referred to in Article 12 has performance indicators including:
a. coverage of women and children victims of violence who receive complaint handling, health services, social rehabilitation services, spiritual guidance services carried out by trained officers within the integrated service unit;
b. coverage of law enforcement from the investigation level to court decisions on cases of violence against women and children;
c. coverage of women and children victims of violence who receive legal aid services;
d. coverage of repatriation services for women and children victims of violence; ande. coverage of social reintegration services for women and children victims of violence.

CHAPTER IV

OBLIGATIONS AND RESPONSIBILITIES

Article 5

Obligations and responsibilities in providing protection to women and children victims of violence are the shared responsibility of:
a. regional government;
b. community;
c. family;
d. parents.

CHAPTER VI

INSTITUTIONAL ARRANGEMENTS

Article 11

1. In the case of implementing protection for women and children victims of violence, the regional government may establish an Integrated Service Center (PPT).

jdih.pakpakbharatkab.go.id - 8 - (2) The establishment and development of PPT is adjusted to the development of priority needs, and regional financial capacity as well as the institutional capacity of personnel in the region.

3. PPT as referred to in paragraph (1) may accommodate institutions/work units that provide services for victims of violence such as P2TP2A, Safe Houses, Child Social Protection Houses, Trauma Center Houses, Women's Social Protection Houses, Shelters and other service activities in accordance with applicable laws and regulations.
4. The form of services carried out by PPT, includes:a. complaints;
b. health rehabilitation;
c. social rehabilitation;
d. legal aid;
e. repatriation;
f. social reintegration.
5. In providing protection to women and children victims of violence, PPT is obliged to:a. provide services as quickly as possible and free of charge to victims; b. provide protection and fulfillment of victims' rights to health rehabilitation, social rehabilitation, repatriation, social reintegration and legal aid;
c. cooperate with certain institutions in providing translators and volunteer assistants needed for victims; d. network with government or private hospitals for treatment and health recovery of victims and cooperate with witness and victim protection institutions, social protection houses or trauma centers owned by the government, community or other institutions for the health recovery of victims;
e. provide convenience, comfort, and safety for victims; f. maintain the confidentiality of victims;
g. provide fulfillment of legal aid for victims;
h. in the case that integrated service delivery is carried out in a networked manner, PPT remains responsible for the entire referral process of services needed by victims.
6. The provision of integrated services as referred to in paragraph (3) is carried out in accordance with SPM.

CHAPTER III

VICTIMS' RIGHTS

Article 4

Every woman and child victim of violence has the right to obtain:
a. respect for dignity as a human being;
b. protection from family, community, Regional Government and/or other parties either temporarily or based on a protection order from the court;
c. health services according to medical needs;
d. special handling related to victim confidentiality; e. assistance by social workers and legal aid at every level of the examination process in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
f. spiritual guidance services;
g. to determine their own decisions.

